I believe the last Sun-3 models (both 68020- and 68030-based) were mfd.
in 1989.  The last kernel for those is SunOS4.1.1 circa Oct. 1990, but
Sun's online (and 800 toll-free) BBS allows one to still download fixes,
patches, etc.

Sun dropped their 386i systems about the same time.

As occurs here in this newsgroup, support for older Sun models continues
in their newsgroups (and in users' groups, etc.).

Sun will still maintain the older systems, and they provide attractive
upgrade paths to their newer hardware.  For example, a Sun-3/60 to Sparc
is simply a motherboard swap and some $$$.

Hey, the world isn't standing still.  New hardware is coming out all the
time as technology advances.

But I still have some 14-year old VAX 11/780 and 785 systems, also a 4000 is
coming soon.  I have one SGI IRIS that's only 3 years old and is totally
obsolete; just traded it in yesterday.  I'm keeping all the Sun-3 systems,
and even bought one for home use, and I'm getting Sparcs as needed, along
with HP boxes too.

Point being: just because a system is technically obsolete doesn't mean
it's incapable of doing useful work.

Sometimes a "field tested" system is a much better investment than the
Latest'N'Greatest.

In fact, I've learned the hard way over the years to NEVER buy a version 1
or a version N.0 of software, and to NEVER acquire the first production of
new hardware.  Let someone ELSE do the unpaid beta testing!  :-)

