As has been mentioned in this discussion before, there is no question about
DEC's past history of supporting product lines long after their technology
is out of date -- doesn't necessarily mean that this behavior will continue.
As I recall Sun announced some time ago (2 yrs?) that there would be no more
software updates for the 68000-based workstations.  I remember joking that
Sun sales would undoubtably claim that the current version was perfect
:-)  Condemning us to Ultrix is practically the same thing.

About 6 months ago I spoke with an HP representative who was trying to
get us to port to thier hardware.  He said that HP was very strong on
using OSF ports directly on thier hardware.  I wonder where they stand
on OSF's backing off thier kernel.

I have heard some hints that this is a smart move by DEC to shift
sales to the more profitable Alpha.  By shifting OSF/1 resources to
Alpha DEC would be effectively telling thier MIPS customers that they
and they alone have decided that the MIPS technology is obsolete.
Customers who determined thier own depreciation and planned
osolescence schedule are being asked to accept an appreciable advance
on that schedule.  Few customers would buy a workstation and plan for
it to last forever, we plan to upgrade based on the technology, not
the Digital profit margin.  After all, we have our own profits to
worry about.  Early Alpha sales should be to people who are planning
to upgrade or increase thier computing facilities.  Those of us
engineers whose employers are smart enough not to give in to this
strategy are stuck with Ultrix.

-- Les Walker


