In article <1992May29.184917.28349@PA.dec.com> grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L. Grant) writes:
>In article <980@niktow.canisius.edu>, pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:
>some, not all.  maryfran johnson, from Computerworld, in an article yet to
>be published, left the impression that OSF/1 didn't run on any DECstations.  
>in the following week, she will print a correction to her article, with a 
>chart of what currently does and does not support OSF/1.

Are you trying to claim that DEC does support OSF on the DECStation? How do
you define support? I run a developer's version of OSF/1, and have been running
various vendor's versions of OSF/1 for over a year.  None of them are
commercial grade products.  OSF/1 shipped by DEC is not currently a commercial
grade OS, either in performance or quality.

There is an ugly secret in this conversation which people are not addressing
because we all have a vested interest in furthering OSF/1.  However, there
is a problem with ignoring ugly secrets: they come back to haunt one.  If
DEC were honest about OSF/1 1.0.x and its problems, then people would
understand why they do not want OSF/1 1.0.x on a MIPS platform.

1) Ask yourself:  What is HP doing about OSF/1?  They also had a strong
   interest in the system.  Why is it that they have stopped pushing
   OSF/1 after having been so excited about it?

2) Ask yourself: What is IBM doing about OSF/1?

3) Ask yourself: Has any other vendor in the world released a OSF/1 based
   system, even though OSF/1 has been released for 1.5 to 2 years?

4) Ask yourself: How is the OSF positioning OSF-MK (a micro-kernel OS based
   on Mach 3.0) versus OSF/1?

5) Ask yourself: What is the research community's impression of the quality
   and performance of Mach 2.5 (upon which OSF/1 is based)?

My personal opinion is that DEC is doing the right thing in cancelling
OSF/1 on the DECStation. I think they should instead add shared library
support to Ultrix.  The people who think they want OSF/1 on a DECStation
should look real hard at the product before they scream for it.  Beware of
what you ask for, you might get it.

Just out of curiosity, what do you think you will get with OSF/1 that
you don't have with Ultrix?

This is an interesting discussion which exactly mirrors the discussion
several months ago on the HP newsgroup in regards to the old Apollo boxes
and OSF/1 support.  HP tends to make good choices.

regards,
fletcher


