Oh, where do I start? 

1) True shared libraries (not the stupid load time shared libraries
others have -- e.g. Sun), links resolved at runtime!

2) real-time support in the kernel (finally!)

3) a much much much better file system: logical definitions to
file systems rather than physical layouts; the ability to 
extend the file system to several partitions. Add partitions
to existing file systems. AFS!!

4) Threads!!! Who else supports this now??? Finally, we will get
multiple threads!

...and the list goes on. Was this a joke question?

Finally UNIX is coming out of the dark ages! But do I expect
this to be as stable as Ultrix? Of course not; but I think
all such leaps need time to stabilize. By the time the MK
arrives, things should be a lot better though.

So now, if I have 20 workstations where I am running a
partcular application, I will *not* be able to take
advantage of runtime resolve of shared libaries, threads
or real-time response. If I do use them, I must throw out
