In article <1992May29.184917.28349@PA.dec.com> grant@glg.ucs.dec.com (Gail L. Grant) writes:

#Open Systems Today, 
#in the 5/25 issue, the title of the article is "DEC Decides No On an OSF/1 
#Port".  in an interview, david stone, vp of software, said that there was no 
#plan of record that we would be offering future versions of OSF on MIPs.  but
#that that didn't mean we wouldn't do it if customers wanted it.  the PR person
#who was monitoring the phone interview interrupted and told the reporter that
#those two quotes went together.  but you look at the article and you have to
#read REAL HARD to see any possibility of OSF on MIPS.  so i say that the press
#does slant things and all i'm trying to do is give you stuff direct from DEC.
#

As the reporter who did that interview, let me add my $.02.

David Stone said the decision had been made not to port OSF/1 to the
DEC line. He then said that the decision was subject to review based
on customer input. I asked him if I should say this was a "tentative"
decision then, he said, no, no, it was much stronger than
"tentative"---but it was subject to review, still.

(We pause for a word now from our sponsor, Webster's Ninth New
Collegiate Dictionary: "tentative: .... 1. Not fully worked out or
developed.... ")

He also spent a good deal of time talking about how this is not the
old DEC that changes its mind a lot about strategy. The decision has
been reached, he said. It's final. It's definite. And its subject to
review based on customer input.

Much head-scratching ensued on this side of the phone line, as I
contemplated the joys of writing a story that delivered DEC's clear,
unequivocal, absolute, fortright and definite "maybe" to our readers.

These are the first two paragraphs of the story that appeared in the
May 25, 1992 issue of OST, based on that interview with David Stone:

"DEC Decides No On An OSF/1 Port"

BY MITCH WAGNER

Maynard, Mass---A high-level Digital Equipment Corp. official said
last week that the company will almost definitely not port OSF/1 to
its Mips Computer Systems-based line.
     David Stone, vice president of software engineering, said DEC is
committed to providing an easy migration path for users from the Mips
line, but the future of DEC systems will be focused on Alpha, not on
the Mips-based computers in the DECstation and DECsystem line. Unless
there is much higher than anticipated user demand, DEC will not port
OSF/1 to existing or future DECstations or servers, he said.....


Now, I think that pretty well sums up the situation, and I don't think
you have to look "real hard" to see any possibility of OSF/1 on
Mips---it's right there in the second paragraph of the story.

I'd like to see some clear, unambiguous statement of direction out of
DEC---none of this, "the decision is definitely made, we're definitely
not going to do it and we're going to be reviewing that based on
customer input." Either a decision IS made or it ISN'T. If it ISN'T
made, there's no shame in saying so---but saying that it is a
"definite maybe" is not the way to produce articles in the "trade
rags" that state your position clearly....




