From everything I have read about ALPHA, it seems as if it was
designed for the utmost in speed and scalability from Day 1 so that
DEC could regain its performance edge in the industry.  Pointing it at
VMS solely seems to have been a bit shortsighted.  If there is one
thing that recent history has taught us it is that UNIX *LOVES* RISC
processors.  The proprietary OS's (VMS, HP's MPE, IBM's MVS, etc) were
designed close to the hardware they were launched upon and can be a
real devil to port to other architectures, RISC being an "other".  HP
learned this lesson, it took them quite a few years to get MPE XL up
to snuff enough for use in the real world.  DEC is learning many of
the same lessons.

