To summarize the discussion below...

	- OSF/1 is Digital's UNIX strategy.
	- Digital is committed to OSF/1 and ALPHA.
	- ULTRIX will continue to be supported and 
          it will support MIPS.
	- Digital is currently assessing OSF/1 support of 
          MIPS based on business and customer considerations.

Steve McIntosh
OSF/1 Marketing Manager

Digital Is On Track With OSF/1
==============================

Digital's UNIX strategy hasn't changed since it was first established
over two years ago.  From the shipment of the first OSF/1 Advanced
Development Kit in March of last year to the release of DEC OSF/1
Version 1.0 in March of this year, Digital continues on track with the
implementation of this strategy. The key elements of the strategy are:

1. Move to a single UNIX operating system based on OSF/1
2. Support multiple hardware architectures with OSF/1
3. Provide for smooth migration of ULTRIX customers to OSF/1.

Single OSF/1 strategy

Two years ago Digital studied alternative UNIX implementations
(including System V and BSD), and chose OSF/1 because the kernel
technology is innovative and because the Open Software Foundation
implements an Open Process for implementing technology.

OSF/1 is based on a modern and extensible MACH kernel which includes
leading functionality such as logical volume management, threads, and
shared libraries.  

The OSF open process attracts the best open systems technologies and
then offers them as compatible industry standards.  This open process
has been well-received by the industry, as evidenced by such popular
technologies as OSF/MOTIF, DCE and DME.

Part of the Digital strategy is to consolidate the BSD & System V UNIX
systems on to OSF/1 by providing compatibility with both in the DEC
OSF/1 product. Digital's UNIX operating system and value-added
investments are being focused on OSF/1 as its strategic UNIX platform
for the 90's.  Digital has already added significant value to OSF/1 in
the DEC OSF/1 V1.0 release by including realtime extensions based on
the POSIX 1003.4 standards, a SVR4 compatibility framework, and a host
of other products for multivendor system and network management and
software development.

Support for multiple hardware architectures

Digital envisioned the need to support multiple hardware architectures
with OSF/1, and planned for this when first developing its OSF/1
strategy. This capability allows for maximum flexibilty in meeting the
platform needs of customers.  To make this work, Digital is focusing on
a single implementation of OSF/1, and is providing underlying
mechanisms for ready support of different hardware architectures.  As a
result, Digital will be able to develop OSF/1 for Alpha quickly, even
though DEC OSF/1 V1.0 was developed and deployed on MIPS-based
DECstations and DECsystems.  OSF/1 on ALPHA is Digital's strategic UNIX
platform.  The question of whether to also offer OSF/1 on current or
any future MIPS-based systems is currently being evaluated based on
business considerations and customer input.

Migrate from ULTRIX to OSF

Since the inception of its OSF/1-based UNIX strategy, Digital has made
a major investment in enabling the migration of customers and their
applications from ULTRIX to OSF/1.  First, a high degree of ULTRIX
compatibility exists in OSF/1 to ensure easy application migration. 
Second, Digital has provided migration services and documentation to
facilitate the process, and additional migration tools and services
will be rolled out over the next few months.

Digital's original migration plan was to step customers first from
ULTRIX on MIPS to OSF/1 on MIPS, and then to OSF/1 on ALPHA when it
became available. Development plans for ULTRIX were and continue to be
centered on quality improvements and coexistence with OSF/1 environments.  

Now--two years later--the Alpha platform is ramping up quickly. 
Understandably, Digital's customers say they want to get to OSF/1 on
Alpha as quickly as possible, and they want an easy migration. 
Although Digital's multi-architecture strategy for OSF/1 has not
changed, the firm's UNIX strategists have accommodated the stepped-up
Alpha timetables by shifting OSF/1 priorities to Alpha.  

In the meantime ULTRIX customers have asked for a performance boost for
their current systems.  Digital will provide that performance with
ULTRIX support for the R4000 daughtercards for the MIPS-based
DECstations and DECsystems.

The priority shift for porting DEC OSF/1 to Alpha, the high level of
compatibility between DEC OSF/1 and ULTRIX, and the decision to support
the R4000 daughtercards with ULTRIX are proof that Digital is on track
with its long-standing OSF-based UNIX strategy.


