In article <1992May26.155820.13838@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu>, mdchaney@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu (M Darrin Chaney) writes:
|> Generally, crashme doesn't work on CISC architectures (3/60 has a
|> Motorola 68030).  It works on RISC architectures by making the processor
|> very unhappy.  RISC processors simply aren't provided with alot of the
|> built-in checks that CISC processors are provided with.  Speed is
|> improved, at the cost of having to hope that bad instruction sequences
|> are never executed.

That's what the author of crash claimed (and it seems, you just repeat 
his claims and prefer to ignore reality ...).

He changed the way crash works several times, attempting to prove 
the superiority of the CISC processors (later the higher degree of maturity 
of the OSs' running on CISCs) without much success.

Some OS bugs were found (and fixed), but not *one* of the RISC 
processors became 'unlucky' because of the lack of 'built-in checks'.


Do you know, what you're talking about ? 
What is a 'bad instruction sequence' ?
(And how does a processor show its emotions ?)


The RISC processors got rid of many architectural complications due 
to their load/store architecture. If there are undefined instructions, this 
doesn't mean the processor will hang, it just means that you aren't 
guaranteed any useful results from this operation. 

CISC processors with complex VM and TRAP handling hardware
are much harder to verify (and correct in case of a problem), than 
RISC processors which often heavily rely on OS handlers for this 
purposes. Its much easier to fix an incomplete kernel subroutine 
than a missing instruction in the CISC microcode.


STefan
