In article <1026@grivel.une.oz.au> norm@neumann.une.oz.au (Norman
Gaywood) writes:
>DECwindows come standard with xhost style authorization. This is too
>insecure for our environment so I've been trying to get the
>MAGIC-COOKIE stuff working. I am hanging around the bottom of the
>learning curve at the moment, so I may be wrong, but: DECwindows does
>not have MAGIC-COOKIE authorization! What are you supposed to use
>instead?

Unfortunately, you're not wrong: DECwindows only supports xhost style
authorization.

Authorization per user is available in X11 release 4 and release 5
(XDMCP protocol), but is NOT supported in the DECwindows clients (dx*).
Moreover, although DECs X server (Xws) has options to enable XDMCP, the
use of xdm is NOT supported by Digital.

We contacted Digital Technical Support and got the following official
answer:
- all dx-clients will stay on X11 release 3 level,
- only xhost style authorization is possible,
- the use of XDM is not supported.

We find this unacceptable and dangerous, and have formally asked DEC to
support XDMCP in the DECwindows environment.

In the meantime we have decided for security reasons to abandon the use
of DECwindows.  If this situation doesn't change in the near future, it
will certainly affect our purchase strategy...

