>Or to summarise: "proprietary operating system" is a tautology.
>(correspondence about the oversimplification here will not be entered into)
 
In response to which lancelot@spock.uucp (Thor Lancelot Simon) wrote in
article <1992May25.203904.15573@spock.UUCP> that he doesn't like SunOS or
NeXT or OSF/1 or VMS or ATT and why didn't I mention ATT and did I know
that "open systems" is a much abused term and I don't have the source code
to Ultrix and lots of people think like me so the things I say might become
true and the claims I made about the systems I develop being "high quality"
"reliable" and "open" are "bullshit" and I have no imagination and can't
recognise quality ... and in general got really upset about lots of issues
not even remotely related to anything I said.  Phew; at least I keep my
insults mild and leavened with smilies.

The closest Thor got to relevance was when he implied that I was spouting
marketing hyperbole; this I feel I can respond to.  

My statements were a rebuttal of the kind of "open systems" marketing
nonsense that regards "open" and "proprietary" as mutually exclusive,
and/or equates "open" with "Unix".  Let me elucidate:

   o  "proprietary" means commercial; something that somebody owns the
      rights to and sells for profit.  "Open" means conforming to agreed
      standards.  The two concepts are independent; a product can be open,
      or proprietary, or neither, or both.

   o  Marketing hyperbole (by definition) concerns *only* proprietary
      products.  This doesn't stop some companies making mystifying claims
      that their products are not proprietary, and even more mystifyingly,
      that this makes them superior to their competitor's products.  Try to
      imagine any other industry where someone could stigmatise a
      competitor by describing their products as proprietary.  Try imagining
      this with the car industry for example --- do you find yourself
      tapping your forehead and saying "tok, tok, tok"?  Now you
      know how I feel.

   o  There is no special relationship between "open systems" and "Unix". 
      Unix is a family of products.  "Open systems" is a philosophy of
      multi-vendor interoperability.  An "open systems" solution is one
      that makes all your equipment work together seamlessly/effortlessly,
      whether or not all of it, some of it, or none of it runs an operating
      system from the Unix family.


