Unix was supposed to be portable, open, and self defense against vendor
changes.

Funny how things work out.  Ken Olsen was ragged to death when he called
the "portability" issue of Unix "snake oil".  Those of us who knew the
truth quietly danced "way to go KEN!".  Ken's snake oil comment has to be
the top Ken-comment with the ISDN quip a close second.

A lot of us old time DEC watchers were very confused by the initial
DECstation announcement.  We had always thought DEC suffered from the
"not re-invented here" syndrome.  Have you worked on an RC25 lately?
Could MIPS have ever lasted?  Whatever well meaning DEC employees may
tell you the market forces are moving and its best to get with the program.

This could very well be the year of the death of RISC.  As much as I would
have liked to see the 100mips R4000 part it isn't here.  We see alpha, but
we also see a pricey tag and a headache to motherboard the thing.  A lot of
former RISC believers sold their stock and no longer preach.  Of course Intel
could still let us down with P5.  I have a feeling that the market will give
Intel extra time but not DEC.  88000 goodbye, SPARC still sputters, i960 gets
some dwindling government money.

What a confused mess.  V.4, OSF/1, MOTIF, X/OPEN, OPEN-DESKTOP, OPEN-LOOK.
While the big Unix boys argued, Bill Gates let Dave Cutler quietly build
their api32 steam roller.  

NT will roll over the "Unix power houses" and blow everything else out of
the market place.  The snake oilers will probably still be fighting over
who is the flatest pancake.

So I'm glad to see DEC mumble about NT on alpha and sign agreements with
Microsoft.  Just think what your migration path might have been.  PINK!
What an incredibly lucky accident that IBM's lovers spat with Microsoft
caused them to jump in bed with Apple.  So Microsoft was without a date
and dorky DEC was standing there all by itself too!  A match made in hackers
heaven.  The table is set and you already know whats for dinner.  If you
can't see the writing on the wall then its time to turn that vt52 off and
go outside.

I saw Gordon Bell's quote that Ken Olsen was the "Fidel Castro" of the
computer industry.  I fell to the floor in hysteria.  I decided that if
Ken was really our "Fidel" than he would have stuck to a V.4 OSF/1 dogma.
Glad to see that he has not.  I know it will upset a lot of customers,
kinda de-ja vu' for some o' us 36 bit types.  Will DECstation guys wear
black arm bands at the next DECUS?

Those who bought the dead "open, portable, vendor independent" systems
that are near death will have the chance to act out their scenario and
port their stuff.  You get to love it AND leave it!

I'll have to be doing some of this work myself.  At least some employment
is left!  The migration to the 486/NT platform will in all probability be
the last migration and un-employ most of us.  Cheers!
-- 
