Ultrix is finished... one last release with some bug fixes and performance
improvements, and then it goes to rot on the shelf.  Now Ultrix is not all
that bad, but in many respects is archaic _already_, so we can hardly hope
to do much useful work with in the years to come.

OSF/1 may not be supported for all MIPS platforms.  Huh?!?!?  DEC "has
not yet decided" which platforms it will support OSF/1 on.
Huh?!?!?!?!?!?!?  I guess DEC has not yet decided whether it gives a
sh*t about its customers either.  Well, while they make up their
collective mind, some customers are making decissions long overdue:
stop bothering to deal with a company has accomplished nothing but
utter confusion in years and go with someone who has shown some
consistency and dedication.  Sun, maybe, or NeXT even.

Maybe Windows/NT will run on all DEC RISC machines some day, but for
now that's not even vapor, and I doubt that I'd ever want to run an OS
from the Microsoft hackworks on machines that an entire institution
relies on.

So what do we do?  Throw out all our DS 3100s?  Run 4 different
operating systems on our DEC hardware alone?  I guess what they are
telling us is: "we're in deep doodoos, but we've figured out a
brillian solution... we'll just make all our loyal customers throw
away all their outdated equipment and buy shiney new Alphas."  Problem
is, the loyalty of those customers has been straining already, but
this will be sure to be the breaking point for many.

Sorry DEC, but just showing off a chip that promises to be a little
faster than the others for a couple of years just doesn't cut it.
You'll have to do better than that to even see blue sky again, never
mind black ink.

I think it only goes to show that you simply can't rely on proprietary
operating system software in the world of "open systems."  The FSF has
it right... I only wish that the GNU OS were here already, so that I
could run one high-quality OS on all our platforms and be assured that
it will be supported and enhanced indefinitely.  Strange, that... I
hear that the open systems industry is a multi-billion dollar
business, but I'm putting my greatest hope in a tiny group of hackers
on a budget that probably doesn't match a single DEC executive's
salary.  Am I confused, or is the world crazy?  8-)
