Unix is the *only* operating system so far that is usable by the academia
AND the industry.  Such end-of-the-world scenarios as above is nothing
but insanity. Alpha is working; light years ahead of *anything* that
intel has or even thinking of developing. It will be competitively
priced and the motherboarding is already done. The storage problem
alone renders 32-bit computing invalid and intel, along with others
are going nowhere. The programmable architecture of Alpha is an
extremely bright solution which has let DEC *binary* convert VAX
programs written in macro. Tell me *one* architecture which is even
3 years way from that!

RISC still holds the price/performance target; and alpha is 1000x scaleable.
I'd say RISC has a lot of life left in it. "Beware the ides of March"
articles are silly and uninformative. 486/NT is a good example of bad
hardware + bad software. The migration to the 486/NT platform is nothing
but a waste of human effort. Unix is a proven solution (although it could
be improved a lot -- OSF/1 2.0 is a good start) and Alpha can be benchmarked
today to be very far ahead of 486. But if you want to invest your money
on the 486/NT dead-horse, go ahead.

