Just a short comment to be thrown into this whirlwind.  I saw it
coming... The MIPS based DECstations were only a STOPGAP.  DEC went
too far with them though... Because in their heart they knew they
would have their own RISC processor just down the road and move to it.
Now look at the mess they are in, their financial situation making
things worse by forcing them to piss of their customers with rash
decisions (or threats of having to make them).  The age of RISC
workstation hardware hit DEC when they weren't ready for it several
years ago so they signed a pact with MIPS so they could say "we have
RISC workstations too!" at that time.  Now, they are pissing off
customers left and right, but it wasn't like they couldn't (or some
within DEC) see it coming when they finished work on their own RISC
processor, now called ALPHA. 

I guess one can't blame DEC for trying to do something at the time
(several years ago), they had to because everyone else was coming out
with RISC based UNIX systems.  

--protective flame-retardant suit activated--

DEC should have had the forthought that HP did when they started
work early on with RISC technology, AND THEN PRODUCED MARKETABLE HARDWARE
WITH IT. DEC delayed its entry into this now very significant processor
technology and forced them to make a compromise they are paying for now
as they huried to develop their own RISC processor.

--protective flame-retardent suite deactivated, DUCK!--

One good example of the DEC/MIPS fiasco: The DECsystem 5800.  When
I heard about that beast iniatially my first reaction was, get a RISC
based MIPS processor to function in a system that was designed from
the ground up for the VAX processor family (XMI,BI bus, etc), the
thing will never work right!  Well, several months later I was
reading in Charlie Matco's DR column how "The MIPS processor wasn't
very happy on the XMI bus, performance isn't as good as it should
be at all".  It was like Frankenstein.  I even hear that each 5800
CPU has its own attached VAX SPU board so that DEC diagnostics can be run.
Now I hear that the 5900 (the replacement for the 5800) has its own SCSI
adapter (on the CPU board I assume) and that the BI bus is not supported
at all, basically you now have a VERY LARGE MIPS/ULTRIX workstation with
very limited I/O expansion.  Ur.... right.  DEC went too far with MIPS...

But unfortunately, due to how long it took them to develop their
own MARKETABLE RISC processor (I know DEC had another internal RISC
effort before ALPHA which was scrubbed), they will now pay the price
from customer disastisfaction, it is a loose/loose situation for DEC.
Either they abandon the MIPS-OSF/1 route and ULTRIX and loose customers
or they maintain two seperate UNIX OS's and suffer large operating
costs for having to run two seperate UNIX operations (ULTRIX and OSF/1,
running on both MIPS and ALPHA).

One possible idea: DEC could sell (or outsource) their ULTRIX
devlopment effort to some other party to unburden themselves from it
and concentrate on OSF/1 on ALPHA.  If they did this, maybe they could
then free up some resources to make OSF/1 run on the MIPS based DEC
workstations and systems.  Maybe to make it all neat and tidy,
sell/outsource the development of the DECstation (MIPS based systems
from DEC) to someone who can further develop /and enhance them.  DEC
can concentrate on ALPHA like it wants to and not worry about the mess
they are sure to be in any way they are pursueing it now.  I know this
idea sounds "easier said than done," but if anyone has a better idea,
lets hear it!  Help get DEC out of a difficult situation and keep the
installed base of MIPS based DEC products at least reasonably happy
and content.  I think DEC's current solution involves "generous"
trade-ins for current systems, but unless it is an outright
zero-charge replacement I doubt this will quell the tide of
discontent.  Especially when one considers the large installed base
of ULTRIX software developed.

Let's stop the flame wars on this subject and perhaps start to think of
useful solutions.  I think everyone more or less knows why DEC is doing
what it is doing.  Its got a hot RISC processor of its own design and
the marriage of convience with MIPS must end for DEC to evolve its UNIX
(and VMS) systems with an architecture that can do it for them well.

