A friend without Internet access has a problem with DEC's Ultrix
TCP/IP-DECnet Gateway.  His problem is getting a telnet/cterm session
with 8-bit characters. He has to support the ISO 8859-1 char code for
getting German characters (the famous umlauts) for some end users.
[or let it be DEC's multi-national char set -- just 8 bit is essential!]

We can get a 8 bit session from some Unix host to the Ultrix machine.
(The details of the mechanics are not nice, but we can get there).
We can trick the Ultrix machine in accepting 8-bit chars at the login
prompt, but the gateway escape (nodename::) instead of (username) is
not recognized, and a login for user (nodename::) is attempted. So: Do
we really need to write our own gateway? How can the gateway be configured
to support 8 bit data?

For your curiosity, let's prentend we use vt220 terminals (if you start
looking out for the various tty types in the tty line config files).


Wanted:  A full 8 bit character terminal session 

   SomeUnixSystem --------------> UltrixGateway ------------------> VMS

                  Telnet session                DECnet terminal session
                     TCP/IP                             CTERM


[and before you ask/suggest: There are a number of VMS-Systems,
and putting some TCP/IP package on each of them is out of the question]


