mike@socrates.umd.edu (Mike Santangelo (UNIX/VMS Sysmgr)) writes:
>Just a short comment to be thrown into this whirlwind.  I saw it
>coming... The MIPS based DECstations were only a STOPGAP.  DEC went
>too far with them though... Because in their heart they knew they
>would have their own RISC processor just down the road and move to it.

	It does look that way, I realize, but I think it's just too
simplistic a response to assume it was a conspiracy.

Basically, we got stuck because of the intersection of two events:
	One: the R4000 was late, and didn't perform as well as it was
		expected to. More accurately, by the time it was ready
		its competitors had caught up to it and it wasn't the
		leading technology everyone hoped it'd be.
	Two: the Alpha turned out to be faster than we'd expected it
		would be. Yields on the chips are also real good.

	The various predictions for R4000 performance versus Alpha,
adjusted for cost of the chip convinced DEC that the MIPS line was
going to be powerful enough to compete without our needing to try
to sell Alphas in the UNIX space - the difference in performance,
which was predicted to be a narrower gap than actually appeared -
didn't justify it. So that's why DEC was saying for a while, "no
UNIX on Alpha" - we fully expected the MIPS-based machines to be
close enough in performance and lower in cost to manufacture that
it wouldn't be necessary.

	What's actually happened is that the R4000 was late and is
not leading the HP and IBM processors now that it's ready, and the
Alpha chips are powerful enough to give some real performance edge.

	What you're seeing is not the results of conspiracy, it's
the result of getting badly jammed by events. Saying that the changes
DEC's strategy have been undergoing are the result of a conspiracy
would imply that the conspirators are absolute lunatics. *NO* company
would deliberately put themselves through this kind of grief.

	This is not really germane - but if you can try to remember
the way the RISC and UNIX market looked about 2 years ago and pretend
you're a big company and you need to develop a strategy for your
future platforms. Now, try to play a little game and figure out what
you'd do, given:
	a) there's third party chips that are coming soon that will
		be binary compatible with your already successful
		product line. they'll be faster than what Sun will
		have - very competitive
	b) there are internal RISC chips in development that will
		be a little faster when the're done
	c) there is a perception that "open systems" philosophy will
		not be overly welcoming of a proprietary architecture
		like Alpha (remember this would probably be before the
		open marketting plans for Alpha were laid down)
	d) ACE on the horizon (I guess it would still have been in
		the back rooms at this point)

	Things always look simpler from the back seat of the car.

mjr.


