Not all current and future permutations, but then we only have the most
common types of machines around here.  In any case, how many machines
does the GNU compiler generate code for now?  More than a dozen archi-
tectures and hundreds of permutations thereof.  Why do you think the
GNU OS will be less well ported and supported than the compiler?

The beauty of free software is that someone out there will have a system
that they want it to run on, so they'll do the necessary work to get it
running.  Especially when the alternative is to use something which not
only costs thousands of dollars in license fees, but which isn't as good
and for which source is not easily available.  So the point is not that
the FSF will support _all_ of this... but somebody always will, and even
if you have to pay someone to do it, you might still be better off.  You
_can't_ pay somebody to fix the latest braindamage in (i.e.) Ultrix... 
you're totally at the mercy of DEC.

In fact, even some hardware vendors will undoubtedly support the GNU
OS in the future... a small vendor of workstations will probably find
it more economical to hire a programmer or two to make sure that GNU
runs on their hardware than to develop something from scratch or pay
huge license and royalty fees to one of the big guys.  Again, precedent
is demonstrated by the GNU compiler... several vendors have customized
it for their needs and are shipping it with their systems.
