>Actually, our little Sun 3/60s with SunOS 4.1 survived it quite
>nicely.  In fact, "crashme" was having a hard time staying alive --
>the operating system seemed to keep trying to kill it.  Something
>about "the best defense is a good offense" comes to mind....

SunOS would have to suck harder than it already does for crashme to kill
a 3/60.

Generally, crashme doesn't work on CISC architectures (3/60 has a
Motorola 68030).  It works on RISC architectures by making the processor
very unhappy.  RISC processors simply aren't provided with alot of the
built-in checks that CISC processors are provided with.  Speed is
improved, at the cost of having to hope that bad instruction sequences
are never executed.

I doubt the OS was "trying to kill it."  It probably just stuck its nose
where it shouldn't have, and died from it (bus error or segmentation
fault).  There's no magic to that.

