I can't resist piping in, especially for those not familiar with the
DEC way of doing things.  Having worked on DEC boxes (though not lately)
and observing DEC over the last 15 + years,  it is clear that the latest 
round of backpedaling on which platforms are going to be supported by 
which OS's is nothing more than business as usual, nay, a tradition at DEC.

You see the way things work when DEC wants to make a major change
is that they announce something absurd and then wait to see which
and how many of their customers scream the loudest.  After an 
approriate amount of time for this feedback, they'll come back and
say "Oh, you misunderstood what we said"  and then go on to present
a much more reasonable approach.  This reasonable approach generally
includes pacifiers for those who have screamed the loudest.

This has been going on for years, one historical example I remember clearly.
I was at a DECUS years ago when DEC proposed a major change in OS license 
transfer policy which effectively prevented users from reselling any of 
their DEC systems.  Talk about stirring up a hornets nest, million dollar
orders were being cancelled on the spot.  Needless to say, DEC backpedaled
within a week.  Other examples ?  DEC's been trying to End Of Life the
PDP-11 series for years, yet it seems like they've recently just come out
with another new PDP-11 model.  'Nuff said.

Point is don't lose too much sleep about the "current" DEC announcements,
just keep on squeaking the wheel you're interested in saving (like what's
been going on in this thread) and if enough users threaten to cancel
orders or jump ship, chances are it'll get oiled.

Oh, and by the way, if you're going to be doing a lot of business with DEC,
you'll need to get used to this method of "market research"; as I said
it's nothing new.  And before I get flamed,  I didn't mean the preceding
as a slam.  It's just that every big company has their own way of doing 
things and the sooner you figure it out what that method is, the easier 
life becomes (fewer ulcers).

