At this point, I can assume that a lot of people are holding off on
current or future expenditures.  This, of course, is wise - until a
real handle can be gained on what DEC will actually do.

The only drawback is that it's a Catch-22.  I am certainly not going
to recommend additional large purchases at this time as I do not feel
it's wise to add additional hardware which may (or may not) be
"orphaned" now or in the foreseeable future.

However, by halting purchases, it sends a signal to internal management,
who are removed from the front lines, that interest in the Mips line
is waning - sales are decreasing, after all!  Therefore, the strategy to
phase it out is a good move.  No sense sticking with a platform that is
less and less popular.

Those who advocate doing what your gut or conscience tells you (whether
that is continuing to purchase or not), but _also_ to express your 
concerns IN WRITING to your sales representative, are those who understand 
the best balance.

Halting purchases does not do the product any good unless you explain
why you have ceased purchasing and also clearly explained whether this
will halt FUTURE purchases.  On the flip side of the coin, however, is
that if you continue to purchase but still have a concern, it is still
in your best interest to explain that to your sales rep, especially if
future DEC action or announcements may temper your willingness to 
continue to do business with them.

As always, words on paper are MUCH more effective and powerful than
a phone call.  Paper can be distributed, filed, reviewed and cataloged.
Phone calls result in a "a lot of people called and..." type of
statements which don't carry as much weight.

Concerned?  Write (or email) your representative(s).  But write it
WELL.  No accusations; no ranting or raving.  See: Diplomacy.

-- 
