    A PD implementation of CAM is not trivial it would take one of the
vendors, an altruistic third party, or an educational institution to be
willing to spend the time and release the code.  There are two large 
parts in a CAM implementation the peripheral device drivers, PDrv's, and
the SCSI interface modules, SIM's.  Are there examples of either or
both parts that would help to get the ball rolling ? 

    Our TRI/ADD group has created an example User Agent, UAgt, application
program.  This program uses the pass through driver, in CAM, to send
CCBs to control a scanner.  They are also working on making a kernel
level peripheral device driver for the scanner.  I will check with them
to see if they would be willing to put this into the PD.

    Examples of SIM's will be more difficult for they are really 
written to control a SCSI Host Bus Adaptor, HBA.  HBA companies like
Adaptec, Future Domain, and Western Digital may have company policies
about the availibilty of the SIM software for their boards.  I do not
know what these policies are.

    There may be a lot more success for peripheral vendors to supply
a PDrv for their own special peripherals.  If it helps to sell their
products it usually is a win.

    (This is not necessarily a plug :-).  We have available, via TRI/ADD
and DECdirect, our CAM kits for V4.2 and V4.2a for just the cost of the
media reproduction.  This is as close to PD as my group can get.  The
kit is an object level kit, however, it is designed to make the addition
of a third party PDrv, like a scanner, printer, or toaster, easier.  We
considered this kit one big bug fix for our old SCSI sub-system.  This
is the most usefull for people on the DEC path, but it is a start.

