Huh?  Though Sun's own kernel was built with the cc that accompanied SunOS
versions up to and including SunOS4.1.2/Solaris 1.0, their stated reason for
including that compiler was solely for kernel rebuilding.  Of course, it
compiles a lot of stuff off the net just fine!  :-)

As for your statement that UNIX has always been accompanied by a cc, not true.

For AT&T's 3B1, the "development set" had to be purchased separately.

For AT&T's 3B2, the "compiler & language tools" had to be purchased separately
(at least as recently as SVR3.2.2 for which I just helped a colleague install
on his 3B2/600).

On the Convergent/UNISYS SVR3 boxes (mightyframe, S4040, etc.) my recollection
is the C development environment was a separate "bundle."

On the Honeywell/BULL UNIX boxes, I believe the C environment was separate.

For SunOS, Sun has sold a more-featureful (my "real" :-) compiler for quite
some time (my catalogs only go back to 1988, but that's at least 4 years now).

The SGI IRIX 4.0.1 (fairly recent kernel) we're running required a separate
bundle for the C programming environment.

I believe our HP-UX boxes were accompanied by a C environment of the same type
as Sun's.

The above aren't exactly "small potatoes" in the UNIX marketplace! :-)

For VAX/VMS, since 1984 I've had to buy Vax C and subscribe for all its
upgrades (up to 3.2 or sumtin' now).

For the DEC-20, I originally bought "Sargasso C", but later switched to the
free KCC from SRI-International (a MUCH better compiler, and was accompanied
by source for both the compiler and libraries).

Sun's unbundling of C from Solaris 2.0 has caused much discussion in the
comp.sys.sun.* newsgroups.  Solaris 2.0 will, of course, cost most than prior
versions of SunOS simply because it IS more: both BSD and SVR4 environments.

Their market analysis indicated (to them) that the vast majority of Sun
users don't use a C compiler (and probably wouldn't even know what one is);
their boxes are and have been going to sites where people plug'n'play
commercial applications; i.e. USERS, not developers.

I'm not happy about that unbundling of the compiler either, but I see it
as an economic necessity for a company operating in the commercial market.

Those who need and/or want a C compiler environment will still be able to
get one.  And not just from Sun; there's a company in Palo Alto CA (name
escapes me at the moment) who is putting together a complete C package
based on GNU C for Solaris 2.0.  And there ARE other 3rd party producers
of C compilers/environments for the Suns.

Looking at my 1988 USENIX catalog (sheesh, I need to update my catalog
library :-), I find the following companies producing C compilers for UNIX
systems:

Oasys, for 68020 systems
Harris Corp. for their UNIX systems
Intergraph, for their UNIX systems
Omron Tateisi Electronics Co.
Scott, Foresman and Company
AT&T
Convex
Green Hills Software, Inc.
Microport Systems, Inc.
Alcyon Corporation
Intrinsic, Ltd.
etc.

and I'm not even going to bother looking at the AT&T, Sun, other catalogs.

Point is: there are a LOT of companies producing after-market C compiler
systems for UNIX boxes, and they have been so doing for years.

Why would that be if a C compiler accompanied ALL UNIX systems?  :-)

