One's feelings regarding "Nuke/Not_Nuke VMS" seem to stem from how one
migrated to VMS.

If one moved up to VMS from MS-DOS, PDP-11 OS's, etc. then VMS would appear
to be a "good" OS.

If one moved down to VMS (from a DEC-20 or other truly good OS's), then
VMS fits the phrase: "Vomit Making System".

Since the early 1960's, I've used over 50 OS's from some 30 vendors, and
have even written two myself (for specialized applications: credit checking
networks for a large retailer, and another for nuclear reactor testing).
Plus I've developed numerous real-time process-control systems.

For years I even thought UNIX was a piece of crap (because I was listening
to people whose opinions I respected (but no more); once I could buy my own
UNIX box, I could see those others were wrong).

As much as I do like UNIX (and I also run AT&T's Silicon Valley UNIX Users'
Group, meeting at the AT&T center in Sunnyvale CA, for the past 3-4 years),
I recognize that it has deficiences, too, and is not perfect.

This forum is not the place to discuss the deficiences of UNIX and VMS,
though I'll state my list of VMS deficiencies is far greather than UNIX's.

From this perspective, I really and intensely dislike VMS.

But I use it (VMS) daily, and have several major commercial products which I
sell to the VMS marketplace; my clients include the world's largest banks,
governments, etc etc.

And the recent acquisition of the CMU/Tek-IP package has at least made VMS
more palatable for me ... it's easier now to move stuff between my UNIX, and
other, systems to/from VMS for software development.  Why DEC didn't have
TCP/IP with VMS from the beginning I'll never know, but it was things like
that which crippled (from my perspective) VMS' usefulness over the years.

Again, from my perspective, I saw VMS as a "production" system and UNIX as
a "development" system (because it (UNIX) has the multitudinous tools which
VMS lacks).

