>	Who said R2000's are going to be "unsupported"? We're not making
>any more of them, but we'll still service them and ULTRIX will still run
>on them and you can still use them for years to come. Software for the
>R3000 machines will run just fine on them, so unless all the ISVs decide
>to abandon our MIPS-based line you've still got a perfectly useable
>workstation.
ULTRIX happens to be one of the most problematic (read: buggy) operating
systems we have ported to.  In the old days, DEC promised that things would
"be fixed in the next release".  For the past six months or more, it has
been "this will be fixed (or will not be a problem) in OSF/1".  Now, we have
to worry if some problems will ever be fixed, much less in a timely manner.
And I doubt that "software for the R3000 machines will run just fine" when ISVs
like us are building new versions on OSF/1.  We can support ULTRIX, sure,
but how soon will it be before there is an R3000-only ULTRIX?  Again, I
doubt that products linked to that OS will work just fine on an R2000 system.
I concur with the many people who believe DEC is making a mistake in now
trying to alienate all of the many people who bought DECstation 2100s and
3100s.  MIPS and SGI still support R2000-based systems with new OS releases.
