It will be 2-3 times slower for scrolling, drawing zero-width lines
and filling rectangles. For everything else there should be little
performance difference. For doing PutImage GetImage it will be
at least 3 times *faster* than the PX. So it all depends on what
you're doing.

>Takes about a second to grab full frame. But dxgrabframes is not
>a fast program (or a good one)

It's certainly not fast. A test I did this morning with a dedicated
program I could grab 26 frames/sec. in 256 x 256 x 8 bit (the greyscale
visual). Just as one data point.

>minimal functionality -- no direct & integrated audio support which

Well, it's a *video* board.

>is simply stupid for a multimedia offering. If I cannot input the
>audio signal & snycronize it together with the image [and output
>it simultaneously]

There are two cases I can think of:

a) you have a external analog video/audio source and want to present
   them in a window with sound. The video needs to go through the machine
   in order to end up in the window, but the audio can just go straight
   to a speaker (which isn't in the machine anyway). This is inherently
   synchronized and what works trivially.

b) You want to capture streams in realtime and store them. This is a
   difficult task in a non-realtime environment and I agree that
   the current XMedia offering doesn't give you enough help in
   doing that easily. We can do it here, but the code wasn't ready
   in time for the first release.

> The DECAudio offering is also a brain damaged product which has a totally
>different way of programming & handling audio [which I think is
>horrible].

Care to explain ? Do you expect to program audio exactly like video ?
Programming with the audio server at the Alib level certainly is
difficult, but then programming with X is difficult at the Xlib
level as well. The audio toolkit makes that a lot easier.


