>>>>> On 22 May 92 15:29:29 GMT, mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. Ranum) said:

> ip_boss@degsyd.syd.deg.csiro.au (Jack Churchill) writes:
> Ok, I'm convinced DECnsr is a good idea.  I have just ordered it.
> Can it be used to back up NFS-mounted disks which are on a VAX/VMS system? 

Marcus> Yes, sort of. I think the 'save filesystem' command doesn't
Marcus> save NFS mounted filesystems (via the scheduled backups and
Marcus> remote invocation) *BUT* the user-level "save" program does.
Marcus> This has come in REAL handy for me in the past, since you can
Marcus> get around machines that don't have NSR set up on them (or I
Marcus> guess supported) by just mounting the filesystem and having
Marcus> cron do a "save -L /mounted/filesystem" every night. Works
Marcus> fine. Should work for a VMS container file, too...

Disclaimer: I don't have any VMS machines to test my suggestion.

During my NetWorker evaluation period, I was curious how to go about
backing up machines that didn't have NetWorker clients written for
them (namely VAX 11/780 running 4.3 BSD and NeXT).

Qualix tech support mentioned the following: a client's NSR resource
contains "save set".  If "save set" is "All", then all local disk
partitions will be backed up.  To allow "savegroup" to back up NFS
filesystems, you had to explicitly list all of the filesystems, *local
and NFS*, to back up.  You can't mix "All" and explict filesystems
(e.g. "All" and "/home/moofles" isn't allowed).

Using this explicit naming scheme, I had NetWorker back up both VAX
and NeXT filesystems without any hassle, save increased net traffic.

I've found that NetWorker generates *a lot* of network traffic --
enough to almost saturate an Ethernet (server parallelism set at 4
simultaneous clients).  NFS backup creates much more traffic.  I
highly recommend NFS mounting the desired filesystem(s) on the
NetWorker server.

-Scott
