eric@telebit.com (Eric Smith) writes:

>What is the difference between a 2060 and a 2065?  One person told me the
>2065 was a dual CPU machine, and another person told me that it was a faster
>2060.
I'm not sure yet, but I believe it was a enahnced CPU. Late in the life cycle,
DEC build an larger Cache Modul, and the paging mechanism was enhanced
by larger hardware tables.

>Does TOPS-20 support multiple CPU configurations?  What's the maximum number
As I know, TOPS-20 never supported multiple CPU's.

>I know there were dual CPU configurations of the DECsystem-10, the 1088 and
>1099.  Did those use MOS or core memory?
They always used the external Memory Bus. DEC built only a CORE memory
for the external Bus (MH10) with 256kwords. (Uhh was really a big thing).
There was an MOS Memory for the External Bus from Ampex, with up to 4Mwords
I believe. But it was very expensive, and I don't think they sold too much
of them. Theoretically, the External BUS would allow for up to four CPU's,
but supported was only up to three. It was true SMP (Symmetrical
Multiprocessing). 

We modified one of our MH-10 memory cabinets, pulled out the core, and
put in homebrew MOS Modules instead. This spent us 1MWord (plus 256kWord
in a core MH10), so we had 1.25 Mwords the last years. This saved us
many many bucks (the alternative was Ampex). The design of the replacement
modules was based on 256k bit DRAMS, and it was expandable, but 1.25 MW
was more than enough for our applications.

>Does anyone have spare maintenance manuals and prints for the 2020 or 2060?
>Or any other model of -10 or -20?
Sorry, we did waste them with our -10 last year.

>Also, is there such a thing as a Massbus to SCSI controller?
No, at least there is one problem, Massbus on the -10/-20 has 18bit!
Also the format on the disk is 536 bytes (128 words).
I did think about such or a similar thing (Massbus - SMD) a few years ago,
but this was too large a project.

Klaus
