In a previous article, eric@telebit.com (Eric Smith) says:

>I have a few questions about the DECSYSTEM-20.
>
>What is the difference between a 2060 and a 2065?  One person told me the

The 2065 is a slightly faster 2060.  It is not a dual processor.  I 
think there was some larger cache and a faster pager.  

>Does TOPS-20 support multiple CPU configurations?  What's the maximum number

I don't believe that this was ever released (or for that matter worked on).
I know TOPS-10 had SMP support in release 7.01+.  Internally during the
development of "jupiter" (faster than the 2065) We heard rumors of a "tops-36"
OS which was a mix of TOPS-10 and TOPS-20.  It may have had it.  Jupiter
died..(sniff)..and we never got to see any TOPS-36.

The multi-cpu version of the TOPS-10 was typically a 1099 (dual KL-1090 
which was essentially a 2060 with perhaps some different microcode).
I know that there were dual KI-10's as well.

The maximum number of processors I remember seeing was like....6?  I think
Oak Ridge and Martin Marietta had setups like these.  Too bad there was a 9 bit
job number or terminal number limitation in the OS!

>Can a multiple CPU configuration use MOS memory, or does it have to be core?
Yes.  There was DEC and third party memory.  Very pricey in those days.

>I know there were dual CPU configurations of the DECsystem-10, the 1088 and
>1099.  Did those use MOS or core memory?

1088 no (unless third party), 1099 yes.  (AMPEX was the name of the third
party memory people).

>Also, is there such a thing as a Massbus to SCSI controller?

I doubt that.  I've seen unibus to scsi (which may be an option if you have
a 2020 and sources).
