On Wed, 20 May 1992 07:25:19 GMT, k2@bl.physik.tu-muenchen.de (Klaus Steinberger) said:
> No, at least there is one problem, Massbus on the -10/-20 has 18bit!
> Also the format on the disk is 536 bytes (128 words).
> I did think about such or a similar thing (Massbus - SMD) a few years ago,
> but this was too large a project.

I've considered that, and the Massbus to SCSI controller would have to do
byte packing, four 18-bit "words" from the Massbus to nine 8-bit bytes on SCSI.
Some SCSI drives can handle arbitrary block sizes like 536 or 1072, so those
would be easy.  Otherwise, the controller would probably either waste some
space, or pack eight 128*36/256*18 Massbus blocks into nine 512 byte blocks on
the SCSI disk.

Cheers,
Eric


