In article <1992May19.164432.20291@rdg.dec.com> dierick@ketje.enet.dec.com writes:
> dxgrabframes produces software compressed video,
> so you can play with the number of frames per second
> and the size of the frame.

Minor nit: dxgrabframes does just that: grabbing frames. It steers
a laser disc player to a frame, grabs the frame and then writes the
it to stdin of a subprocess. The default subprocess is one that
produces software compressed video, but you can override that.
The format is PBMPLUS :-). We have seen rates of 34 frames/min.
in 640 * 480, 24 bit color on a 5000/200, end-to-end (i.e. no meaningless peak
number but what you really get when you read the data and do
something with it), which translates to about 30 MByte/minute for
the dxgrabframes combo as shipped.

You can get a *lot* faster when you write your own program and do
the processing in the same application as the grabbing (the above
forks a new process for every frame and calls a script). Rates
that we have seen were up to 15 frames/sec. in 320*240, 8 bit greyscale,
(again, end to end, the stuff then was compressed and written out,
but that code is not on the XMedia tape). I can make the frame grabbing
part of that available. That rate translates to about 65 MByte/min.,
so I would assume that is the limit.


