So far this thread has dealt mainly with the operating systems.  As a
long standing VMS customer that had migrated to Unix, I'm very annoyed
with a patter in DEC where products come out first for VMS and then
the Unix (whether Ultix or OSF) is promised for much later.  

If you look closely at DEC's latest glossies advertising that "DEC
does it all" for microcomputer networking, you'll see that file
service to Macintosh is still VMS only.  

There are a number of things that VMS does well -- perhaps even better
than Unix.  Why is DEC making no visible effort making them available
to Unix.  If nothing else, it would help people move back and forth
between the two worlds.  In there absense, small companies like BBC
(Boston Business Computing) have shown that it's quite feasible to
port these utilities to Unix.  

I'll believe that DEC is serious about Unix when they release a series
of products for Unix and then announce that the VMS version will be
available in 12 to 24 months.


