ldg@scott.skidmore.edu (Leo D. Geoffrion) writes:
>
>There are a number of things that VMS does well -- perhaps even better
>than Unix.  Why is DEC making no visible effort making them available
>to Unix.

	That's one of those long-term-strategic-kind-of-questions
that I'm definitely not qualified to comment on officially. As a
long-time UNIX user (who never has used VMS except where I had to)
and former [competitor deleted] and [competitor deleted] customer,
I never felt a big need for the functionality of VMS.
	A lot of the niftier features of VMS are starting to show
up in the UNIX space - things like OSF's DCE should go quite a
ways towards making UNIX more like "clusters" or giving similar
functionality.
	One of the big problems you have with providing these kinds
of technologies with UNIX is in actually being able to use them
across your entire UNIX platform set. Back when I ran a mixed
shop of Suns and a Gould PowerNode, the Suns all had NFS and
we thought it was wonderful, but NFS being new, we didn't have
it for the PN. Basically, the machine was suddenly demoted
to useless. I shudder at the idea of being a systems manager
asked to provide a "cluster" across 50 machines made by 5
different vendors, some of which are running totall different
revs of operating systems.
	This isn't just a Digital problem - it's only recently
that people are even trying to solve those kinds of problems
in the commercial UNIX space. Hopefully DCE with IBM and HP
and DEC committed to it, will be able to provide a stable
enough set of agreed-upon-functionality that all this stuff
will play together.

>I'll believe that DEC is serious about Unix when they release a series
>of products for Unix and then announce that the VMS version will be
>available in 12 to 24 months.

	Nonono... you've got it wrong! We should be shipping the stuff
whichever one is ready sooner, and not *CARE*. In fact, that's becoming
more and more the case - tools like VUIT, which we ship for VMS, ULTRIX,
and I forget which other UNIXes...

	I realize that to the outsider it looks like there's a "ship it
on VMS first" attitude - that has been giving way very fast to a "ship
it when and where it's ready" attitude. I think it's a step in the
right direction. [Obviously we shipped TPU for VMS first, and the
ULTRIX version took a little longer - but it's there now. ;)  ]

mjr.


