I think the thread in this topic has finally found the right path. 
The question we should be asking is not whether DEC is serious about
Unix but whether ALL the major computer companies are serious about
Unix and how they are all going to work together in mixed sites (like
ours where we have DEC/Ultrix, VAX/VMS, SGI, Sun).  It's pointless
trying to blame any one company for not providing the solutions to all
our problems if they go to waste when another make of computer is
suddenly added to the network.  In fact, I feel DEC always had an
excellent system of integration for their own hardware, particulary
for VMS systems (clustering is just one of their good examples).  Of
course their solutions are expensive but for those single-vendor big
sites where money is not a big problem, it doesn't matter since
results take greater priority.  So, I wish the major computer
companies work on a total "unix" solution.  I haven't seen a lot of
evidence to prove this is actually going to happen.  Is it too simple
to ask?  Is OSF the answer?  If it is then what happens to all the
sites using different unix's on Suns, SGI, HP, etc., etc?  Is OSF
going to end up as yet another operating system working along side all
these others?  Or will OSF replace all of them?  Do I sound
frustrated?  Not really, just a little mad at the slow progress in
operating systems over the years compared to layered products and the
hardware itself.

