ldg@scott.skidmore.edu (Leo D. Geoffrion) writes:

>I'll believe that DEC is serious about Unix when they release a series
>of products for Unix and then announce that the VMS version will be
>available in 12 to 24 months.

Well, how about TCP?  :-)

Seriously, DEC is in a "damned if they do, damned if they don't"
situation here.  Two of them, actually.

First, no matter which is announced first, the other side is offended.
Only simultaneous announcment seems acceptable.  But in many cases,
that's not the best way to develop a product.  If 90% of the market for
it uses VMS, why hold put ULTRIX development resources into it at all?

Second, there are a number of places where VMS and ULTRIX clash (or so
I'm told by some VMS weenies :-)).  To modify ULTRIX or provide
solutions which don't match the general UNIX/Ultrix usage model will
lessen Ultrix or the solution in the eyes of the Unix community.  DEC
is trying to balance two ultimately irreconcilable goals -- move the
VMS folks to Ultrix by providing VMSlike facilities in Ultrix, but keep
Ultrix in the UNIX mainstream.

The long-term strategy is to hope that Unix/Posix/OSF move towards some
solution which both VMX and Ultrix can use, thereby making it a
non-issue.  But that time-scale is probably measured in decades.
-- 
Simmons' Law Of Diskless Workstations:  "For every dollar you spend on
  a diskless workstation, expect to spend a dollar on a file server."
Corrollary: "If you spend $.75 instead of $1.00, expect to spend another
  $.75 fixing it when it doesn't perform to spec."


