====================================================================
(THE STATEMENT BELOW IF FROM THE OPEN SOFTWARE FOUNDATION)

                     Statement re: OSF/1
           OSF's continued commitment to OSF/1 technology
                         (May 8, 1992)

OSF is unequivocally committed to the OSF/1 operating system, and
intends to aggressively pursue its on-going development and adoption in
the marketplace.

There are currently two parallel streams of development activity for
the OSF/1 technology:  OSF Engineering in Cambridge, MA continues its
work on the current generally available, Mach-based integrated kernel
version (OSF/1); advanced development work on the OSF/1 MK microkernel
version of the technology, available in snapshot form to OSF members
only, is being carried out concurrently under the auspices of the OSF
Research Institute.  OSF plans to preview a convergence strategy for
the two parallel development efforts into a single, microkernel-based
next-generation OSF/1 operating system with its membership at its
upcoming Members Meeting.

OSF's standard engineering model, which is geared to facilitate early
adoption and accelerate the enhancement of OSF technologies, includes
sharing the engineering effort with OSF technology suppliers and
licensees.  This has been our model for the initial integration and
development of all  OSF technologies, and continues to be the model for
on-going development and enhancement, as appropriate, for OSF
technologies, including Motif, DCE and OSF/1.

Expanding the on-going development effort to include the third party
development efforts is a cost-effective way to strengthen and expedite
OSF technology growth and adoption.  It does not, in any way,
constitute any kind of de-commitment, or diminished commitment, by OSF
to any of its family of open systems technology offerings.


