The symptom is that certain tapes cause BACKUP to report
(to the batch job's .LOG file):  
  %MOUNT-F-DEVOFFLINE, device is not in configuration or not available
This is followed by a message to the operator console:
  %BACKUP-I-READYWRITE, Mount volume 5 on _HECNB$MSA0: for writing 
  Answer "YES" when device is available

The error log shows a device timeout.  The tape drive is moving
the tape from the load point foil until it reaches the end-of-tape 
foil.  It seems to me that the drive is failing to make sense of the
label field, and will search to the end of medium trying to find
a label.

The same tape shows the same error on a separate, identical
system.  If the tape is mounted by a DCL $MOUNT/OVER=ID
the TSV05 drives will time out.  The same command on a TU81 tape
returns 'tape is not labeled' and the error log shows a read
error.

I recently discovered that if the BACKUP has a fatal tape error, in
which the drive must be powered off and on to clear the error, the
next tape to be mounted will be rejected (timeout) even if it is
already initialized.  After the mount attempt on the TSV05, the
label is gone (seen by mounting that tape on a TU81).

I have made three calls to Central Support, with the advice of
'call field service.'  Field Service has been out several times.
All diagnostics pass.  TSDRIVER.EXE is at the proper
level (X-21) for VMS5.4-3.  Firmware is up to date.

An independent VMS consultant says it is a well-known problem of
the TSV05.  What does the net say?  (I'll post summary - Thanks
in advance.)

