>Watch out for arguments to functions where the argument is not
>explicitly declared (and typed).  You should explicitly declare the
>formal parameters to routines or their sizes may not match up with the
>caller (default is "int" which truncates 64-bit addresses).

If you're programming in C, there is no excuse for not using function
prototypes.  ANSI C with prototypes has been standardized 1989.  As good
programming practice, you should be using function protoypes on any new
code you write.  It would also be a good idea to add them to existing
code (who knows what bugs you might find?).

Presumably proper prototyping will help you DTRT for Alpha C compilers.
