This question is directed towards the DEC folk who are nice enough to
read this newsgroup. Soon after these interesting "DEC's commitment to UNIX"
series of messages in comp.sys.dec, I read the following in the May 11, 
1992 issue of Digital News, Page 4:

  "Although Digital stated its intention to make OSF/1 the operating system
  for MIPS-based DECstations when it joined the ACE initiative a little more
  than a year ago, it is now re-evaluating whether OSF/1 will go on to the 
  MIPS platform, according to a DEC official."

  "Our future Unix strategy is OSF/1 on Alpha [DEC's own RISC architecture],"
  said Steve Mcintosh, a DEC OSF/1 marketing manager....

I am aware of DEC OSF/1 V1.0 for MIPS which started shipping in February,
and was marketed as the "developers version".

I know of DEC OSF/1 V2.0 for MIPS which should provide support for all
DECstation/DECsystem platforms (especially PXG systems) and become the 
"end-user" O/S by the end of the year. 

Is there any truth to the Digital News story? Is DEC really
"re-evaluating" the offer of OSF/1 V2.0 to the "end-users"?
I certainly hope not! I agree that DEC should concentrate on ALPHA OSF/1,
but before engineering efforts stop on the MIPS line, I'd like to 
see the completed version of OSF/1 on those platforms. We've waited long
enough for features that did not get implemented in Ultrix since they
would be in OSF/1 (read Shared Libraries).

Thanks

