cazier@mbunix.mitre.org (Cazier) writes:

>One question is why is Ultrix not clusterable? Will OSF/1 correct this?

	UNIX in general isn't - it wasn't designed with "clustering" type
things as an original design goal. A lot of factors have prevented vendors
from (until recently) trying to tackle making stuff like clusters work,
mostly interoperation issues with other UNIXes. Consider that Apollo had
some pretty advanced features for coupling UNIX machines together - nobody
but Apollo had 'em and they're dying out. It tends to drive the costs of
the operating system up, too, and the UNIX space is prone to some pretty
sharp price wars. Also, to really do clustering right in UNIX, you'd want
to be able to handle clustering across different architectures, with
different byte orders and world lengths... It gets tricky quickly.

	We're starting to see a lot of trends towards clustering-like
features, what with OSF's DCE, and the Andrew file system, etc. IBM is
claiming to do clustering, but there's still (to my knowledge) no
process migration or any of that.

mjr.


