bill@falcon.cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:
> ...
> We have decided to standardize all our twisted pair terminal and printer 
> connections to the layout used by DEC for their MMJ connectors.  
> ...

We also have used the MMJ pattern in our RJ45 connectors.  The
advantage to this pattern is that respective signals (e.g. dtr/dsr,
transmit/receive) are symetrical and so it's easy to make a 
"null modem" (flipped) cable or straight-thru cable.  The disadvantage
of this pattern is that the respective signals do not fall within the
pairs of wires in a standard rj45 patch panel.

The answer to "so what" on this -- the decserver 90tl and decserver 700
use rj45 with a pattern that differs from the MMJ pattern but conforms
to the twisted pairs within an 8 conductor cable when connected
to a patch panel in a standard at&t layout.  

So if you're at the beginning of standardizing on a pinout pattern
and have not done so yet, you might consider these other patterns.

