Three little factoids in the discussion whether DEC is commited to Unix
(discussion restricted to the segment of the market I am interested in,
fast-CPU desktop and deskside systems):
1. The only Unix machine of any flavor I can buy from DEC today
   (vaporware doesn't count) is a MIPS-based DECstation/DECsystem. Ultrix
   no longer exists for VAXstations (at least last time I inquired about
   it for our 3100s it wasn't available any longer).
2. The current Ultrix is a dead end; it has been announced that 
   4.3 will be the last upgrade, with the product going into maintenance
   mode (we all know what that means) afterwards.
3. For the current hardware, there will be no new operating system
   development (from the trade rags, OSF/1 will be implemented on Alphas
   only). Therefore I conclude that that the hardware will be frozen soon
   (and in the tough market of today, that means it will die out within
   a year or so).

In summary, the current software offering is a dead end, the current
hardware offering is a dead end. Now for the department of vaporware,
according to the trade rags the emphasis in OSes for the Alphas is VMS
and Windows/NT (maybe the rags I read are biased, but OSF/1 is always
mentioned in the fashion of "by the way, there will be an OSF/1 too").
For the second time in 5 years, DEC's Unix custumers will have to
switch to another platform (and this time they switch both hardware
and software, going from BSD-based to OSF); many may choose to switch
vendors while they are at it. Is that a commitment to Unix, and
support for the customer base? Not in my book, more a "me too".

Will the Alpha turn that around (after all, I don't care about the "me
too" attitude, if the product is so hot in performance and
price/performance that I can't ignore it)? I don't think so. The
machines scheduled to be released this fall will probably clock about
80-120 SPECs. The first few months it will be impossible to get them
(proof: track record from the 3100s and 5000s, and the announced
intent to first deliver to software developers). HP has announced a
similarly fast machine (I vaguely remember the number 7100), which is
a compatible upgrade to current machines, and will probably be
available in quantity about a quarter to half year earlier than Alpha
boxes. I presume IBM is not far behind, and NeXT is working on the
dial 88100 machine. Therefore, customer demand for Unix on Alphas will
be low (the people who need this performance can get it from several
other vendors at similar prices); and due to the lack of forceful
demand for OSF/1 on Alphas there will be a lack of implementation
quality and speed. Oh well, seems DEC is out of it.

Note: My employer may or may not have an opinion on this, and I don't
know what it is, nor is this it.

-- 
