Yeesh.  Normally I'm the first guy to complain, but this is just
getting too out of sync.  For the record -- I've been working on
DEC equipment since '80.  My first employer was (and I do not exaggerate)
led down the garden path and then hung out to dry on the Jupiter, so
I have historical reasons to take DEC position statements with a very
large grain of salt.  I know lots of DEC folks on a purely social
basis, a purely professional basis, and both.  My current employer
has done the bulk of his processing on DEC boxes running UNIX since
1982.  The previous employer had a VMS shop and a UNIX shop, so I got
to see DEC in action there too.  We regularly get non-disclosured out
the wazoo on what DEC is doing with Ultrix.  Based on all of that
I say:

DEC is unequivocably committed to UNIX.  The exact flavor(s) they
will be supporting over time (OSF vs Ultrix) will be dictated by
market forces, but there is zero chance of their backing out of
the UNIX market.  They've seen the sales of DecStations vs VAXStations,
they've seen the (non)success of the big boxes running VMS, they've
seen the increased difficulty of getting new accounts to start with
VMS vs the decreased difficulty of getting them to start with Unix.

FYI, you can s/DEC/HP/ in most of that paragraph.

My opinion on the long-term future (10 years):

For VMS, the handwriting is on the wall.  One by one the proprietary
multitasking systems are falling away -- take a close look at Unisys,
and you'll see what the future is for companies which don't go to
POSIX fast enough.  DEC, like HP, knows they *have* to go to Unix.
Killing TOPS-10/20 was wrenching for both sides, and it looks to this
viewer like DEC learned something from that fiasco.  As VMS supports
more and more POSIX features and the VMS features get built into
DECs unix, they will eventually reach the point where VMS can be
put out to pasture.
