  As a "proud owner" of one of the very first 5810s of the assembly line and
  as someone who has corresponded with Michael and other 58nn owners over the
  past two years, I believe that I understand his comments very well.  The
  following excludes some "hard" information that I shouldn't repeat.

  1. the 5810, at time of introduction, was advertised as an "18 MIP" system
     with good i/o and ultimate upgradeability to a multi-processor system[1].
     While "MIPS" is not a very good measure of anything, DEC is usually reas-
     onably conservative with this number.  In this case, DEC was way off base;
     our own benchmarks put it in the same performance range as a VAX 8550
     running ULTRIX, e.g., a 6-7 VUP system [2];

  2. when the multiprocessor version appeared, users encountered various and
     sundry problems.  I won't go into this here, other than to say that there
     was some discussion about them on the net;

  3. during discussions with DEC prior to the purchase of our machine we, at
     least, were led to believe that it, like its VAX 6nnn "cousin" (a 5810
     is a VAX 6nnn with a MIPS processor board instead of the usual VAX proces-
     sor board(s)), would ultimately be upgradeable with future faster proces-
     sors.  This never happened and, for various reasons, is not likely to[3];
     
  4. some of us managed to get rid of our 5810s relatively quickly and thus
     relatively painlessly.  The rest are "stuck';

**5. a few 58nn owners have told me that they are reasonably happy with their
     systems, so the negative view of them is NOT universal. ****

  So the primary problem is that, for many (I believe the majority) of us, the
  58nn is, for many reasons, considerably less of a system than was advertised
  and at the current time, cannot be upgraded in the way that one can upgrade
  a DEC 5000 or virtually any SUN or HP system. 


  ("Trade/rumor rag" notes:

   [1] DEC publications from announcement date to system availability date
   [2] internal benchmarks consisting of "real world" applications executed
       on DEC 5400, 5810, 8550, uVAX II, 3100, 5000/200, as well as systems
       from HP, MIPS, and SUN . 
   [3] architectural problems in the 5810 discussed, in limited fashion, on
       the net.  For more than that, a nondisclosure is probably required)


  I think that what surprises me most about the 58nn - and is a "sore point"
  with some DEC salespeople that I have talked to who know about this system -
  is that DEC continues to offer it.  My personal, subjective belief is that
  doing so will adversely impact DEC's reputation in the long run.


