to! We're trying to make sure as much of the environment's available at
day one - I think most people will be very pleased.

	There's a big difference between code translation and interpretation
as I'm sure you know. The translators compile the VAX binary (or MIPS binary)
into executable Alpha instructions, rather than an interpreter which would
simply evaluate each instruction always. The net effect is a whole lot
better. Translated executables run at the speed o the Alpha, not at the
speed of an Alpha pretending to be a (VAX|MIPS).

>  Do you have a similar facility for ULTRIX people ?  How about some equivalent
>  (pseudo-equivalent) effort from DEC up front, whereby I could move a direc-
>  tory full of source from a MIPS/ULTRIX to an ALPHA/OSF 1 machine and execute
>  make with an "ULTRIX" flag ?  Too much work ??  More than it took to build a
>  translator for VMS ???

	Porting between OSF/Alpha and OSF/MIPS and ULTRIX/MIPS should be
about as hard as recompiling applications was between a machine running
4.1 and 4.2, unless your code is assembler or something like that. The
compilers are all there and they're the same frontends. What more do
you need?

	If you are an ISV, and feel that moving to Alpha is going to
be more than just a recompile, Digital has Alpha porting centers available.
Talk to your sales rep for details. The UNIX ports are pretty trivial,
unless there are machine language optimizations or other nonportable constructs
(EG: applications using Nbuffered I/O, assuming pointer sizes are the same
as the size of an int, trying to share 64 bit longs with 32 bit machines,
kernel dives, and so forth)

	You should be able to take a source tree and type "make" and it
will work. Read my lips: "source code compatible"

	Even if that's not enough for you, there's a MIPS ULTRIX binary
translator too, for the folks who have either lost their sources or who
used assembler, or who knows what else.

	I hope Digital has addressed the UNIX portability issues to your
satisfaction. Most of the time I think we tend to talk about the VMS
side of the Alpha migration because it's going to be a little harder
for them - the UNIX folks should just have a recompile.

	There are presently machines inside DEC that have GNUemacs
and all those goodies running just fine on Alpha. Those aren't ports,
those are recompiles right out of ULTRIX source trees.

>> If you have a UNIX program, and it's a reasoably portable bit of code
>> (none of this sizeof(char *) == sizeof(int) stuff, mind you)
>> then you've covered platforms #3, #4, #(you left out Alpha OSF) and maybe
>> even POSIX/VMS and #7, SCO.
>> 
