>  across UNIX platforms.  Or are they just stupid, not realizing that if they
>  simply kept their "sizeof"s straight, all that work would go away ???  And
>  Larry Wall is just blowing smoke with his elegant Perl installation scripts?

	Larry Wall's elegant installation scripts are to handle installs
between versions of UNIX that have totally different and incompatible
file trees, are BSD or SystemV based, are otherwise bizarre or slightly
different. The operating system interface - all the libraries and stuff
are as close to ULTRIX as humanly possible. Obviously, all the system
call interfaces are the same - or binary compatibility wouldn't work
at all.

	I can appreciate your cynicism and I can appreciate someone
being hard to convince but I can't do anything more to convince you that
this stuff is real than describe how it works and some of the steps
Digital has taken to make it work. No other UNIX vendor that I know
of has gone to such lengths to support porting across a total kernel
re-vamp (EG: new kernel, not upgrade) by providing binary compatibility,
or to provide the source compatibility we have between the MIPS/Alpha
OSF and ULTRIX versions. Frankly I think engineering's done a pretty
amazing job - and frankly I think it shows that DEC has a hell of
a commitment to UNIX.

