In article <1992May07.121723.20183@donau.et.tudelft.nl> wolff@neuron.et.tudelft.nl (Rogier Wolff) writes:
>Now this is an interesting thing the drive automatically loads and executes
>software on a tape! This would make a nice hole to use for a virus: It
>spreads through the tape drives.

In August 1991 I identified this vulnerability with the Exabyte
8500 and the ability to reprogram the firmware from a "load" tape.

Exabyte, CERT, and others are aware of this.  The problem is not
exclusively with Exabytes.  Other hardware, including some 4mm
(DAT) drives are reprogramable in the same way.

In addition to the obvious - trashing of your tape drive's firmware
(hence further productive operation), an infected drive could
compromise the data you read or write.  In some configurations an
aberrant drive could cause intentional disruption or damage to data
on other devices on the SCSI bus.

To make matters worse, the reprogramming takes place immediately
when you insert the tape, and there is no provision for a safety
switch to prevent reprogramming.

For better or worse, Exabyte has freely distributed the firmware
and several utility programs.  It makes diagnosing and reloading
an Exabyte easier, but also puts these tools into the hands of
those who want to make mischief.
--
