Date: Sat, 9 May 92 17:36:08 GMT
Lines: 49

I am evaluating several network products to be used in LAN/WAN environment.
The products currently being evaluated are:

        Banyan VINES
        Novell NetWare
        Novell NetWare Lite (?)
        Pathworks for DOS
        Pathworks for Windows (?)
        Pathways for DOS
        PC/TCP

I have practical experience with some of the products, others only marketing
glossies, and others no information at all.  I don't expect one product to
provide a universal solution.  I am soliciting practical information on each
product--what works, what doesn't, what's real, what's vapour, etc.  I am
also interested in interoperability between the products--same set of questions
plus which products can concurrently co-exist on the same platform, how readily
can data be moved between environments, etc.

Also of interest is how readily the product may support an application specific
environment at the same time and interoperate with VMS, Unix, and various
IBM operating environments.  In the WAN environment TCP/IP or TP4/IP will
be used to connect the LANs.  SMTP will be required for electronic mail.

The current platforms to be supported are primarily IBM PC compatibles with
isolated groups of MAC and NeXT systems.  The immediate need is for electronic
mail, disk serving, file serving, and printer services.  These services should
be available to DECstations, VAXstations, Sun workstations, etc.  They should
also be available to users directly connected to VAX and IBM systems.

The immediate need is to support typical office automation products from
Microsoft, WordPerfect, Lotus, Borland, etc. with some engineering applications
such as AutoCAD.  The long term goal is to eliminate one or more computer
platforms from the desktop--up to 4 platforms are required by some users.
In this light, terminal emulation is also required.  Full VT2xx/VT3xx/VT4xx
terminal emulation and IBM 327x is desired along with a fully functional
X-window server is desired.

Almost forgot that I was interested in SoftPC interoperability with the above
products.  I guess I'm not asking for much, am I?  ;-)

I'll summarize the results to the net; however, don't expect anything before
the middle of next month.  Hopefully, I will get some thoughtful responses
that I can use to balance out any personal biases that I have.  Thank you in
advance.

Merton Campbell Crockett




