>In article <keithl.703861421@mukluk.cc.monash.edu.au> keithl@mukluk.cc.monash.edu.au (Keith Lewis) writes:
>
>Yes, I'm utterly curious about suid programs, and "root" ownership.
>What is to prevent someone with an appropriate disk drive to twiddle a
>few bits on a floppy and create suid root shells and such, mount and
>execute them? Enquiring minds want to know. In the far-flung future I

You can specify a nosuid mount option.  Since mount(2) is only callable
by root, normal users cannot run mount(1) unless it is suid to root. 
So, you make a setuid to root program / shell script (remember about 
the - after the interpreter name), that runs mount(1) or calls mount(2)
and mounts the floppy nosuid.  Normal users can't mount floppies
with setuid enabled, and it isn't a problem.
     


