The nosuid flag for mount.

>At the moment, there doesn't seem to be anything preventing them, 
>other than physical security of the systems' floppy drive. I wonder 
>if a tape could be similarly rigged--dump a script to it with tar
>or cpio, dd the image in, twiddle a few bits, dd it back out, and
>tar/cpio it back in. I don't believe either of them clears suid
>bits, or doing a system restore after a crash would be miserable.
>

You are correct in stating thattar does not clear ownership / permissions, or 
suid bits, when saving files.  

However :   

Chown(2) is only executable by root, so  a normal user cannot extract a 
file from a tar, suid or not, and have it owned by some one else. 

Chmod(2) is only executable by the owner of a file, or root, 
so no normal user can make someone else's file suid either.

So, a normal user can extract suid programs from tape owned by
him, or owned by others and have them become owned by him,
but cannot create files owned by anyone else, let alone
suid files.

>Ack. Nothing is safe; there's no security--better get a few bags of
>concrete and encase my systems.... :-)

Of course there is some security.  You just have to know about
it by reading TFM and when that doesn't work because of the shoddy
